TI  - Spaceflight associated neuro-ocular syndrome: proposed pathogenesis, terrestrial analogues, and emerging countermeasures.
AU  - Ong, Joshua
AU  - Tarver, William
AU  - Brunstetter, Tyson
AU  - Mader, Thomas Henry
AU  - Gibson, C. Robert
AU  - Mason, Sara S.
AU  - Lee, Andrew
T2  - The British journal of ophthalmology
AB  - Spaceflight associated neuro-ocular syndrome (SANS) refers to a distinct constellation of ocular, neurological and neuroimaging findings observed in  astronauts during and following long duration spaceflight. These ocular findings,  to include optic disc oedema, posterior globe flattening, chorioretinal folds and  hyperopic shifts, were first described by NASA in 2011. SANS is a potential risk  to astronaut health and will likely require mitigation prior to planetary travel  with prolonged exposures to microgravity. While the exact pathogenesis of SANS is  not completely understood, several hypotheses have been proposed to explain this  neuro-ocular phenomenon. In this paper, we briefly discuss the current hypotheses  and contributing factors underlying SANS pathophysiology as well as analogues  used to study SANS on Earth. We also review emerging potential countermeasures  for SANS including lower body negative pressure, nutritional supplementation and  translaminar pressure gradient modulation. Ongoing investigation within these  fields will likely be instrumental in preparing and protecting astronaut vision  for future spaceflight missions including deep space exploration.
