AU  - Kholwadwala, Anokhi
AU  - Kadipasaoglu, Cihan M.
AU  - Lee, Andrew G.
T2  - Neurologic clinics
AB  - In this review, we discuss current hypotheses on the pathophysiology of spaceflight-induced neuro-ocular syndrome and recent findings regarding  microgravity-induced changes in venous, lymphatic, and glymphatic flow. We  propose that spaceflight-associated neuro-ocular syndrome (SANS) may not be  explained entirely by intracranial pressure changes only and instead is likely  multifactorial including cephalad fluid shifts and fluid outflow obstruction as  possible inciting mechanism for the characteristic signs and symptoms of this  syndrome. In all, we urge a multifactorial view of SANS whereby predisposing,  intrinsic, and extrinsic factors must all be considered.
