T2  - International journal of molecular sciences
AB  - Understanding the transcriptomic impact of microgravity and the spaceflight environment is relevant for future missions in space and microgravity-based  applications designed to benefit life on Earth. Here, we investigated the  transcriptome of adult and neonatal cardiovascular progenitors following culture  aboard the International Space Station for 30 days and compared it to the  transcriptome of clonally identical cells cultured on Earth. Cardiovascular  progenitors acquire a gene expression profile representative of an early-stage,  dedifferentiated, stem-like state, regardless of age. Signaling pathways that  support cell proliferation and survival were induced by spaceflight along with  transcripts related to cell cycle re-entry, cardiovascular development, and  oxidative stress. These findings contribute new insight into the multifaceted  influence of reduced gravitational environments.
