AU  - Kubota, Kazumi
T2  - Life sciences in space research
AB  - Exploration-class missions and expanding civilian spaceflight increase the need for reliable health protection under constraint-limited rescue bandwidth,  dependence on devices and protocols, and high consequence for small failures.  Spaceflight stressors act as a bundled ecology (microgravity, radiation,  circadian disruption, and isolation/confinement) producing integrated, aging-like  vulnerability across immune, sleep-cognitive, and deconditioning domains. Yet  discussions of space health system design often treat nursing as downstream  implementation rather than a safety-critical discipline responsible for early  warning, prevention bundles, escalation pathways, and human factors-compatible  workflows. This Opinion argues that nursing should be integrated explicitly into  space health operations as a mission assurance capability, with defined  competencies and standards that specify what is monitored, how thresholds are  set, how false alarms and uncertainty are managed, and how deterioration triggers  structured escalation. As a secondary benefit, the same design logic maps to  constrained care on Earth-particularly homebound older adults dependent on remote  support-where reliability depends less on technology availability than on  engineered workflows that prevent avoidable harm.
