TI  - Oxidative Stress and the Kidney in the Space Environment.
AU  - Pavlakou, Paraskevi
AU  - Dounousi, Evangelia
AU  - Roumeliotis, Stefanos
AU  - Eleftheriadis, Theodoros
AU  - Liakopoulos, Vassilios
T2  - International journal of molecular sciences
AB  - In space, the special conditions of hypogravity and exposure to cosmic radiation have substantial differences compared to terrestrial circumstances, and a  multidimensional impact on the human body and human organ functions. Cosmic  radiation provokes cellular and gene damage, and the generation of reactive  oxygen species (ROS), leading to a dysregulation in the oxidants⁻antioxidants  balance, and to the inflammatory response. Other practical factors contributing  to these dysregulations in space environment include increased bone resorption,  impaired anabolic response, and even difficulties in detecting oxidative stress  in blood and urine samples. Enhanced oxidative stress affects mitochondrial and  endothelial functions, contributes to reduced natriuresis and the development of  hypertension, and may play an additive role in the formation of kidney stones.  Finally, the composition of urine protein excretion is significantly altered,  depicting possible tubular dysfunction.
