AU  - Yang, Liu
AU  - Wang, Rui
AU  - Long, Ren
AU  - Ren, Qiushi
AU  - Yang, Xing
AU  - Lu, Jiabin
T2  - Science China. Life sciences
AB  - The smooth transportation of substances through the brain extracellular space (ECS) is crucial to maintaining brain function; however, the way this occurs  under simulated microgravity remains unclear. In this study, tracer-based  magnetic resonance imaging (MRI) and D(ECS)-mapping techniques were used to image  the drainage of brain interstitial fluid (ISF) from the ECS of the hippocampus in  a tail-suspended hindlimb-unloading rat model at day 3 (HU-3) and 7 (HU-7). The  results indicated that drainage of the ISF was accelerated in the HU-3 group but  slowed markedly in the HU-7 group. The tortuosity of the ECS decreased in the  HU-3 group but increased in the HU-7 group, while the volume fraction of the ECS  increased in both groups. The diffusion rate within the ECS increased in the HU-3  group and decreased in the HU-7 group. The alterations to ISF drainage and  diffusion in the ECS were recoverable in the HU-3 group, but neither parameter  was restored in the HU-7 group. Our findings suggest that early changes to the  hippocampal ECS and ISF drainage under simulated microgravity can be detected by  tracer-based MRI, providing a new perspective for studying microgravity-induced  nano-scale structure abnormities and developing neuroprotective approaches  involving the brain ECS.
