TY  - JOUR
TI  - A no-fault risk compensation approach for radiation risks incurred in space travel.
AU  - Cucinotta, Francis A.
AU  - Schimmerling, Walter
T2  - Life sciences in space research
AB  - In this paper we recommend an appropriate compensation approach should be established for fatality and disabilities that may occur due to space radiation  exposures of government or industry workers. A brief review of compensation  approaches for nuclear energy and nuclear weapons development workers in the  United States and other countries is described. We then summarize issues in the  application of probability of causation calculation and provide examples of  probability of causation (PC) calculations for missions to the International  Space Station and Earth's moon or for Mars exploration. The main focus of this  paper follows with a recommendation of a no-fault approach to compensation with  the creation of appropriate insurance policies funded by employers to cover all  disabilities or fatality, without requiring proof of causation or restriction to  conditions that imply causation. Importantly we propose that the compensation  described should be managed by recourse to private insurers.
