TY  - JOUR
TI  - The Earth-Disconnect Phenomenon as a Psychological Stressor for Martian Crewmembers.
AU  - Kanas, Nick
T2  - Aerospace medicine and human performance
AB  - From Mars, the Earth will appear as an insignificant dot in the heavens and two-way communication with family and mission control can take up to 44 min. The  resulting impact of these restrictions on crewmembers may be termed the  Earth-disconnect phenomenon. The literature suggests that seeing the Earth as a  beautiful orb in space is an awe-inspiring experience (the Overview Effect), the  loss of which may produce isolation and loneliness. Not being able to communicate  with family and mission control in real time may produce emotional distress and  impaired performance. Strategies to help crewmembers deal with these issues  include using a telescope or virtual reality to better experience the Earth and  suggesting future response topics and using more formal messaging techniques to  improve communication efficiency. More study needs to be done using simulators, a  space station, or the far side of the Moon to better understand this phenomenon.  Kanas N. The Earth-disconnect phenomenon as a psychological stressor for Martian  crewmembers. Aerosp Med Hum Perform. 2025; 96(6):530-532.
