TI  - Focal adhesions are involved in simulated-microgravity-induced basilar and femoral arterial remodelling in rats.
AU  - Jiang, Min
AU  - Lyu, Qiang
AU  - Bai, Yun-Gang
AU  - Liu, Huan
AU  - Yang, Jing
AU  - Cheng, Jiu-Hua
AU  - Zheng, Ming
AU  - Ma, Jin
T2  - Canadian journal of physiology and pharmacology
AB  - Recent studies have suggested that microgravity-induced arterial remodelling contributes to post-flight orthostatic intolerance and that multiple mechanisms  are involved in arterial remodelling. However, the initial mechanism by which  haemodynamic changes induce arterial remodelling is unknown. Focal adhesions  (FAs) are dynamic protein complexes that have mechanotransduction properties.  This study aimed to investigate the role of FAs in simulated-microgravity-induced  basilar and femoral arterial remodelling. A 4-week hindlimb-unweighted (HU) rat  model was used to simulate the effects of microgravity, and daily 1-hour  intermittent artificial gravity (IAG) was used to prevent arterial remodelling.  After 4-week HU, wall thickness, volume of smooth muscle cells (SMCs) and  collagen content were increased in basilar artery but decreased in femoral artery  (P < 0.05). Additionally, the expression of p-FAK Y397 and p-Src Y418 was  increased and reduced in SMCs of basilar and femoral arteries, respectively, by  HU (P < 0.05). The number of FAs was increased in basilar artery and reduced in  femoral artery by HU (P < 0.05). Furthermore, daily 1-hour IAG prevented  HU-induced differential structural adaptations and changes in FAs of basilar and  femoral arteries. These results suggest that FAs may act as mechanosensors in  arterial remodelling by initiating intracellular signal transduction in response  to altered mechanical stress induced by microgravity.
