TI  - BMP9 attenuates microgravity-related disuse osteoporosis by modulating TGFβ and BMP signaling.
AU  - Zhao, Haoyang
AU  - Yang, Mengfan
AU  - Liu, Yujiao
AU  - Tu, Xiaolin
AU  - Shao, Gaohai
T2  - NPJ microgravity
AB  - Disuse osteoporosis, caused by mechanical unloading, is linked to dysregulated TGFβ and BMP signaling. This study explores their roles and evaluates BMP9 as a  potential therapy. A hindlimb unloading (HLU) mouse model was used to assess bone  changes and signaling alterations. In vitro, a Rotary Cell Culture System (RCCS)  with 3D printing simulated microgravity. BMP9 was overexpressed in bone marrow  stromal cells (BMSCs) and osteocytes treated with TGFβ1. HLU mice showed reduced  bone density, microstructural integrity, and dysregulated signaling (increased  p-Smad2/3, decreased p-Smad1/5/8). BMP9 overexpression restored osteogenic  differentiation in vitro and improved bone properties in vivo. However, RCCS  failed to replicate osteogenic inhibition, likely due to shear stress. Despite  challenges, BMP9 shows promise for treating disuse osteoporosis. Future research  will refine vector specificity and reduce immunogenicity for clinical  application.
