TY  - JOUR
TI  - Sex-specific protective effects of angiotensin II type 1 receptor knockdown on disuse muscle atrophy in the rat soleus muscle.
AU  - Yoshihara, Toshinori
AU  - Takaragawa, Mizuki
AU  - Dobashi, Shohei
AU  - Naito, Hisashi
T2  - Journal of applied physiology (Bethesda, Md. : 1985)
AB  - Skeletal muscle disuse leads to atrophy. Accumulating evidence suggests that angiotensin II type 1 receptor (AT1R) contributes to sex-dependent catabolic  signaling. However, the direct role of AT1R in skeletal muscle is unclear. This  study investigated whether selective suppression of AT1R expression in the rat  soleus muscle via adeno-associated virus serotype 9 (AAV9)-mediated short hairpin  RNA (shRNA) delivery could mitigate hindlimb unloading (HU)-induced muscle  atrophy, and whether this effect differed between male and female rats. Male and  female rats received intramuscular injections of AAV9 vectors encoding  AT1R-targeted shRNA into the soleus muscle. After 7 days of HU, AT1R gene copy  number (digital PCR), receptor abundance (ligand-binding assay), muscle fiber  cross-sectional area (CSA), and downstream molecular markers (including  phosphorylated Smad2/3 and Smad1/5/8, HDAC4 expression, and the atrogenes, Fbxo32  and Trim63) were assessed. Female rats exhibited higher AT1R gene copy numbers,  which were selectively reduced by AAV9-shRNA. Ligand binding confirmed reduced  receptor abundance in both sexes. CSA loss attenuation was observed exclusively  in females, particularly in type I fibers. In female animals, AT1R knockdown  substantially increased Smad1/5/8 phosphorylation and decreased HDAC4 expression.  The AT1R copy number was positively correlated with Fbxo32 and Trim63 expression,  independent of AAV dose. AT1R plays a sex-specific role in disuse-induced muscle  atrophy, as muscle-specific AT1R knockdown conferred selective protection in  female rats. The effect appears to be mediated via Smad1/5/8-HDAC4 signaling  rather than oxidative stress or autophagy. This study provides mechanistic  support for sex-informed therapeutic strategies targeting the muscle-localized  renin-angiotensin system.NEW & NOTEWORTHY In this study, we demonstrate that  muscle-specific knockdown of angiotensin II type 1 receptor (AT1R) via AAV9-shRNA  selectively protects female rats against disuse-induced soleus muscle atrophy.  The protective effect was restricted to type I fibers and associated with  enhanced Smad1/5/8 signaling and reduced HDAC4 and atrogene expression. These  findings reveal a sex-specific mechanism in local AT1R signaling and identify a  potential therapeutic target for disuse atrophy with consideration of biological  sex.
