T2  - Cell biochemistry and function
AB  - Ample proof showed that non-coding RNAs (ncRNAs) play a crucial role in proliferation and differentiation of osteoblasts and bone marrow stromal cells  (BMSCs). Varied forms of biophysical stimuli like mechanical strain, fluid shear  stress (FSS), microgravity and vibration are verified to regulate ncRNAs  expression in osteogenic differentiation and influence the expression of target  genes associated with osteogenic differentiation and ultimately regulate bone  formation. The consequences of biophysical stimulation on osteogenic  differentiation validate the prospect of exercise for the prevention and  treatment of osteoporosis. In this review, we tend to summarize the studies on  regulation of osteogenic differentiation by ncRNAs beneath biophysical  stimulation and facilitate to reveal the regulatory mechanism of biophysical  stimulation on ncRNAs, and provide an update for the prevention of bone  metabolism diseases by exercise.
